A 38-year-old male with severe Crohn disease has been maintained in clinical remission on oral azathioprine (2 mg/kg/day) for 18 months, with normal complete blood counts. He presents to his primary care physician with acute podagra in his left first metatarsophalangeal joint, and synovial fluid aspiration confirms acute gouty arthritis. His physician initiates long-term urate-lowering therapy with oral allopurinol (300 mg daily). Three weeks later, the patient presents to the emergency department with high fevers, severe sore throat, bleeding gums, petechiae, and profound prostration. Laboratory evaluation reveals life-threatening pancytopenia: absolute neutrophil count = 150/microL (normal: 1,500-8,000/microL), platelet count = 14,000/microL (normal: 150,000-450,000/microL), and hemoglobin = 6.2 g/dL. Bone marrow biopsy reveals severe hypocellularity with profound marrow aplasia.

Trace the complete 5-step pharmacological, enzymatic, and biochemical sequence that caused this life-threatening drug interaction:
1. The non-enzymatic cleavage of azathioprine to produce the active purine antimetabolite 6-mercaptopurine (6-MP);
2. The normal endogenous catabolic degradation pathway responsible for clearing 6-MP and its primary enzymatic clearance pathway;
3. The molecular target and mechanism of action of allopurinol on this catabolic clearance pathway;
4. The resulting intracellular diversion of 6-MP into the purine salvage pathway (via HGPRT) generating toxic cytotoxic thiopurine nucleotides (6-thioguanine nucleotides, 6-TGN);
5. The fundamental cellular mechanism of thiopurine-induced bone marrow aplasia and why a mandatory 75% dose reduction of azathioprine/6-MP is required when co-administering allopurinol.

