A 56-year-old male presents to the urgent care clinic with an exquisitely painful, swollen, erythematous left knee. Sterile arthrocentesis yields cloudy synovial fluid containing 68,000 leukocytes/microL (88% neutrophils). Polarized light microscopy with a first-order red compensator reveals numerous intracellular and extracellular, needle-shaped crystals displaying strong negative birefringence (bright yellow when parallel to the compensator axis, bright blue when perpendicular), confirming an acute flare of gouty arthritis. To control severe inflammation, the physician prescribes oral colchicine alongside an NSAID.

Trace the complete 5-step molecular, biophysical, and immunological sequence of this acute gout attack and its targeted pharmacological termination by colchicine:
1. The thermodynamic and biophysical trigger that precipitates monosodium urate (MSU) crystallization within the synovial joint fluid;
2. The mechanical and innate pattern-recognition event (TLR2/4 and the NLRP3 inflammasome) inside resident synovial macrophages upon phagocytosing MSU crystals;
3. The resulting proteolytic cytokine maturation (caspase-1) and secretion that orchestrates massive endothelial activation and neutrophilic chemotaxis;
4. The positive feedback amplification loop of neutrophil recruitment, phagocytosis, lysosomal rupture, and local tissue destruction that drives the excruciating pain of podagra/gout; and
5. The precise subcellular molecular target and mechanism of action of colchicine, explaining how it abruptly disassembles the cellular machinery to extinguish neutrophil extravasation, chemotaxis, and inflammasome assembly.

